<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.3.1: Creating a Pictorial Representation of Exponential Growth</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01267</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.3.1: Creating a Pictorial Representation of Exponential Growth</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2f0a0e0c-3d66-4c18-be19-18a56a25f3ac</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>This opening warm up gives students an opportunity to create a pictorial representation of exponential growth. The purpose of this warm up is to help students make sense of the situation coming up in the next activity, where they are not explicitly asked to make a pictorial representation.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para><figure><media alt="Bacteria viewed through a microscope."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ea0740cee3bc362e73f50595dd33a7bb8b001eb6"/></media></figure></para>
<para>There are some bacteria in a dish. Every hour, each bacterium splits into 3 bacteria.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> This diagram shows a bacterium in hour 0 and then hour 1. Draw what happens in hours 2 and 3. </item>
  <para><figure><media alt="The picture shows 1 bacterium (an irregular polygon with a double outline and light blue inside) for hour 0, then an arrow to the right, then 3 bacteria for hour 1."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3b0c4f4a934fc485c117e8f9697531ab47731d55"/></media></figure></para><title>Student Response</title>

    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 1. See sample drawing.

    <figure><media alt="Illustration of bacteria growth."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/320bfc4508c74501060b4ed241af8ef602222ebe"/></media></figure><item> How many bacteria are there in hour 2? 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 9 bacteria in hour 2
  </item>
  
  <item> How many bacteria are there in hour 3? 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 27 bacteria in hour 3
  </item>
</list>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Invite a few students to share their drawings and their observations about how the number of bacteria is growing.</para>
</section>
</content>
